          "section_context": [
            "Get Started with Datadog"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "What happens to existing application keys",
          "heading": "Get Started with Datadog",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "ul",
            "li",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "navigation",
          "section_context": [
            "Get Started with Datadog"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "How to choose the right authentication model for your use case",
          "heading": "Get Started with Datadog",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "ul",
            "li",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "navigation",
          "section_context": [
            "Get Started with Datadog"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication",
          "heading": "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication",
          "tag": "h2",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h2"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The original Datadog authentication model separated telemetry ingestion from API authorization. API keys identified the Datadog organization receiving data, while application keys identified the user performing an action. This dual-key model worked well when user identities mapped to individual developers. However, this model did not map cleanly to automated workflows such as CI/CD pipelines, which do not have a single human owner.",
          "heading": "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "AI agents acting on behalf of human users introduced another challenge for the existing model. When the Datadog MCP Server calls an API to retrieve a monitor’s status, how do we determine who authorized the request, what scope was granted, and how long that access should last? Security and compliance teams also need to know exactly who accessed what data, and when that access occurred. Application key usage alone does not provide enough context for detailed audit attribution.",
          "heading": "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "To address these challenges, Datadog is introducing authentication models designed around specific access patterns. Instead of relying on a single credential type for every workflow, teams can now use credentials tailored to developers, automation systems, cloud-native workloads, and delegated application access. In the following sections, we’ll look at how each authentication model works and where it fits best.",
          "heading": "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Why Datadog is updating its API authentication"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
          "tag": "h2",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h2"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Our updated authentication model replaces the application key with four targeted alternatives, each designed for a specific access pattern.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows",
          "tag": "h3",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h3"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "PATs are designed for developers who directly interact with Datadog APIs. They are intended for user-driven workflows such as building CLI tools, writing scripts against the API, or pulling data from Datadog into custom applications and dashboards.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "PATs are scoped to the permissions of the user who creates them and require an expiration window to avoid reliance on long-lived credentials. Because PATs are tied directly to a user identity, Audit Trail logs can attribute API activity to a specific user. When a user loses access to an organization, their PATs are revoked alongside their account access.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Personal Access Tokens: for developer workflows"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents",
          "tag": "h3",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h3"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "SATs are designed for automation systems and non-human workloads that need persistent access to Datadog APIs. Common examples include CI/CD pipelines, Terraform runs, infrastructure automation, and autonomous AI agents operating without a human in the loop.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "SATs are tied to a service account, not an individual user. The service account has its own permissions, independent of any individual on your team. Like PATs, SATs are scoped and support configurable expiration policies. Unlike old application keys, SATs include readable prefixes ( ddsat_ ) and identifiable public portions, making credentials easier to track and manage.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Service Access Tokens: for automation and autonomous AI agents"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads",
          "tag": "h3",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h3"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "If your workload runs on AWS, it already has an IAM identity . Workload identity federation, a system that establishes trust between two parties for user authentication and resource access, enables AWS workloads to authenticate directly to Datadog by binding an AWS IAM role to a Datadog service account. Instead of creating and storing static Datadog credentials, the workload uses its existing cloud identity to access Datadog APIs.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "This removes the need to store API credentials in secrets managers, CI/CD pipelines, or configuration files for workloads such as Terraform runners and the Datadog Agent. Teams can manage access through existing IAM policies rather than rotating and distributing long-lived credentials.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Workload identity federation is generally available for AWS workloads, with support for additional cloud providers planned for the future.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "Workload identity federation: for cloud-native workloads"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access",
          "tag": "h3",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h3"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "OAuth clients are designed for AI agents that need temporary, scoped access to Datadog APIs. OAuth supports both delegated access , where an application acts on behalf of a user, and machine-to-machine (M2M) access for autonomous systems operating without a human in the loop.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In delegated workflows, users explicitly approve the scopes an application or AI agent can access before the application receives a short-lived token. This gives organizations more granular control over who authorized the request, what permissions were granted, and how long that access should last.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "For autonomous systems, Datadog is actively investing in M2M OAuth support built around dedicated agent identities rather than long-lived shared credentials. OAuth clients also provide a foundation for emerging interoperability standards such as Okta Cross-App Access, helping AI agents operate across enterprise tooling without accumulating standing access. OAuth client support is planned for release later this year.",
          "heading": "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model > OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "Introducing Datadog’s new API authentication model",
            "OAuth clients: for delegated and temporary AI agent access"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "What happens to application keys?",
          "heading": "What happens to application keys?",
          "tag": "h2",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "h2"
          ],
          "category": "heading",
          "section_context": [
            "What happens to application keys?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Application keys will continue to work after Q3 2026, but they will be considered legacy features and no new capabilities will be added. There will not be a forced migration or any immediate impact on existing integrations. Our new authentication model covers almost every use case application keys previously handled, with better scoping, clearer identity, and proper time-to-live (TTL) support. We encourage customers to migrate to the new authentication model as soon as possible to begin taking advantage of these added benefits.",
          "heading": "What happens to application keys?",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "body",
          "section_context": [
            "What happens to application keys?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Note: Some existing integrations will continue to require application keys. Datadog partners are encouraged to update their code for PAT compatability as we migrate to this new model.",
          "heading": "What happens to application keys?",
          "tag": "p",
          "ancestry": [
            "html",
            "body",
            "main",
            "div",
            "section",
            "div",
            "div",
            "aside",
            "div",
            "p"
          ],
          "category": "navigation",
          "section_context": [
            "What happens to application keys?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "How to choose the right credential for your use case",
